<section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow"><span></span>Practical trust</p><h1>Clear boundaries before participation begins.</h1><p>AAB is designed to support scientific understanding while keeping ownership, authority, uncertainty and responsibility visible.</p></section>
<section class="launch-section"><div class="launch-heading"><p class="eyebrow dark"><span></span>Plain-language commitments</p><h2>What AAB does—and what it does not do.</h2><p>Detailed agreements will vary by country, institution and jurisdiction. These principles describe the public foundation that should remain true in every governed deployment.</p></div><div class="truth-list"><article><strong>Scientific authority</strong><p>AAB may organise evidence, expose contradictions and propose questions. It does not approve scientific truth, regulated activity or real-world execution.</p></article><article><strong>Institutional data</strong><p>Participation must define ownership, permitted use, access, jurisdiction and retention before operational data enters a governed workspace.</p></article><article><strong>Evidence provenance</strong><p>Claims should remain connected to their sources, context, review status, limitations and lineage rather than becoming detached summaries.</p></article><article><strong>Uncertainty</strong><p>Missing information stays missing. Scenarios remain distinct from facts. Contradictions and negative outcomes are preserved for review.</p></article><article><strong>Access and identity</strong><p>An account is not authority. Roles, country scope, institution membership and protected access are resolved from controlled records and fail closed when uncertain.</p></article><article><strong>Expansion</strong><p>New domains, countries and capabilities require explicit governance, evidence and accountable human approval; they are not activated automatically.</p></article></div></section>
